@@ -0,0 +1,1416 @@
+//! `HostVm` on Windows: Mach VM's calls, as core makes them, over sections and placeholders.
+//!
+//! Windows cannot remap an address range's pages to another address as `mach_vm_remap` does, so
+//! a view of guest pages ("virtual memory layouts") maps their sections instead, as Linux's maps
+//! their files (`os/linux/vm.rs`): each run of the source's bytes is mapped over the target from
+//! the section and offset the backing table names for it ([`super::backing`]), and the view is
+//! recorded there as runs of its own. A view of a view maps the section too, so it keeps showing
+//! the pages after the first view goes, as Mach's aliases do.
+//!
+//! **Placeholders.** Windows maps nothing over a live mapping (no `MAP_FIXED`), and unmaps a
+//! view only whole. So every range the library maps is made of placeholders (`VirtualAlloc2`
+//! with `MEM_RESERVE_PLACEHOLDER`): address space that only a view put in its place takes
+//! (`MapViewOfFile3` with `MEM_REPLACE_PLACEHOLDER`), which an unmap leaves a placeholder again
+//! (`UnmapViewOfFile2` with `MEM_PRESERVE_PLACEHOLDER`), and which is cut in two
+//! (`VirtualFree` with `MEM_PRESERVE_PLACEHOLDER`) and joined again (`MEM_COALESCE_PLACEHOLDERS`)
+//! at any page. A view over a placeholder starts at any page of its section, where a plain view
+//! starts only at a multiple of the allocation granularity (64 KiB), so a view of the guest's
+//! 4 KiB pages maps each run of them where it lies. No other thread's allocation lands in a
+//! placeholder, so a range keeps its addresses through every change.
+//!
+//! - **`allocate_anywhere`** is a view of a fresh section of its own, backed by the page file
+//!   (`CreateFileMappingW` of `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`), zero-filled, over a fresh placeholder. A
+//!   view can be made of it, as of any allocation of Mach's, so [`allocate_aliasable`] is the
+//!   same range, where Linux's anonymous memory needs a file's instead. The section's bytes are
+//!   charged to the system's commit when it is made, though a page takes memory only once it is
+//!   touched: a reservation core makes to map views over is charged its length until the views
+//!   replace it.
+//! - **`allocate_fixed_overwrite`** and **`remap_shared_overwrite`** replace what the target
+//!   shows ([`replace`]): each of the kernel's views that meets the target is unmapped, what of
+//!   it lies outside the target mapped again from the same section pages, with the protections
+//!   it had, and the target's new pieces mapped in the placeholders left. The pages outside the
+//!   target are the same pages afterwards, but between the unmap and the map their addresses
+//!   show nothing, where Mach's and Linux's calls never unmap them: a thread that reads or
+//!   writes them in that window faults. A view made at once of several runs is a view of the
+//!   kernel's for each, so a later call over some of them unmaps none of the others. Every byte
+//!   of the target must lie in a range the library mapped (`KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS` otherwise, as
+//!   no placeholder can be made at a page that is not a multiple of 64 KiB), where Mach maps
+//!   over anything; core overwrites only ranges it allocated.
+//! - **`remap_shared_overwrite`** fails with `KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS` where a byte of the source
+//!   has no section behind it, as Mach fails for an unmapped source, before it maps anything. A
+//!   piece the kernel refuses fails the call with the target as it was: what it showed is mapped
+//!   back, the same pages.
+//! - **`protect_max`** is `VirtualProtect`, of each run of the range's pages, with Mach's rule
+//!   for a maximum protection kept by the table: a protection is lowered and never raised again
+//!   (`KERN_PROTECTION_FAILURE`), as Mach refuses, where Linux's `mprotect` raises it. It changes
+//!   no mapping: a view protected in part stays one view. A forbidden write is an access
+//!   violation, where Mach's is a `SIGBUS`: a death either way.
+//! - **`deallocate`** unmaps what of the range the library mapped and frees its address space;
+//!   pages of it that nothing of the library's maps are left as they are, and the call answers
+//!   success, as Mach's does over a hole. A page freed inside a 64 KiB granule is address space
+//!   no later allocation takes while the granule's other pages stay.
+//! - **Whole pages**, of the host's size (4 KiB, `GetSystemInfo`; not the allocation
+//!   granularity), as Mach takes them (`crate::os`'s `the_vm_spans_its_calls_as_mach_does`):
+//!   `protect_max` and `deallocate` take every page the range's bytes touch;
+//!   `allocate_fixed_overwrite` and `remap_shared_overwrite` take the length rounded up to whole
+//!   pages from the page of the address, the remap's source from the page of its first byte too
+//!   ([`length_pages`]), and the remap answers the target's page. A call of no bytes changes
+//!   nothing, wherever it starts: the remap's is `KERN_INVALID_ARGUMENT`, the others' success.
+//!
+//! **Views.** Windows counts no mappings against a limit of the process's (Linux's
+//! `vm.max_map_count`), so the views take no budget and `HostVm`'s counts of them answer none.
+//! A section the system refuses for want of commit is refused as memory is
+//! (`KERN_NO_SPACE`, counted as the host's memory running out: [`section`]).
+//!
+//! **Not measured here**: what an unmap and a map of the same section pages under a device's
+//! import of them does to the import (`VK_EXT_external_memory_host`) on each driver; the runtime
+//! is told of every page a call of core's changes ([`crate::remap`]).
+
+use std::ffi::c_void;
+use std::os::windows::io::{AsRawHandle as _, FromRawHandle as _, OwnedHandle};
+use std::sync::Arc;
+
+use crate::core::num::{ToU64, ToUsize};
+use crate::core::platform::os::vm::{HostAddr, HostVm, KernReturn, VmProt};
+use crate::core::raise::death::{DeathId, DeathKind, fatal_with_detail};
+
+use super::api;
+use super::backing::{self as files, Backing, Piece, READ, READ_WRITE, Run, Table, WRITE};
+
+/// `KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS`.
+const INVALID_ADDRESS: KernReturn = KernReturn(1);
+/// `KERN_PROTECTION_FAILURE`.
+const PROTECTION_FAILURE: KernReturn = KernReturn(2);
+/// `KERN_NO_SPACE`.
+const NO_SPACE: KernReturn = KernReturn(3);
+/// `KERN_INVALID_ARGUMENT`.
+const INVALID_ARGUMENT: KernReturn = KernReturn(4);
+/// `KERN_FAILURE`.
+const FAILURE: KernReturn = KernReturn(5);
+
+/// `ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED`.
+const ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED: i32 = 5;
+/// `ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER`.
+const ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER: i32 = 87;
+/// `ERROR_INVALID_ADDRESS`.
+const ERROR_INVALID_ADDRESS: i32 = 487;
+
+/// The most runs `deallocate` unmaps under one hold of the table's lock, which every resolve of
+/// an import's pages and every other call of the VM's waits on: a view of the guest's scattered
+/// pages is a view of the kernel's a run, and a surface's has thousands.
+const DEALLOCATE_RUNS: usize = 256;
+
+/// The host's page size (`GetSystemInfo`'s `dwPageSize`), read once.
+pub(super) fn page_size() -> u64 {
+    static PAGE: std::sync::OnceLock<u64> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();
+    *PAGE.get_or_init(|| {
+        let mut info = std::mem::MaybeUninit::<api::SystemInfo>::zeroed();
+        // SAFETY: the call fills the one `SYSTEM_INFO` it is given, and cannot fail.
+        let info = unsafe {
+            api::GetSystemInfo(info.as_mut_ptr());
+            info.assume_init()
+        };
+        match info.page_size {
+            0 => 4096,
+            size => u64::from(size),
+        }
+    })
+}
+
+/// The whole pages `len` bytes at `addr` lie in: the first byte of the first, and their length;
+/// `None` where they pass the end of the address space. Those `protect_max` and `deallocate`
+/// take (module docs).
+fn pages(addr: HostAddr, len: u64) -> Option<(u64, u64)> {
+    let mask = page_size() - 1;
+    let start = addr.addr().to_u64();
+    let first = start & !mask;
+    let end = start.checked_add(len)?.checked_add(mask)? & !mask;
+    Some((first, end - first))
+}
+
+/// The pages a call of `len` bytes at `addr` takes where Mach takes the length rounded up to
+/// whole pages from the page of the address (`allocate_fixed_overwrite`,
+/// `remap_shared_overwrite`; module docs): the first byte of that page, and the length; `None`
+/// where they pass the end of the address space.
+fn length_pages(addr: HostAddr, len: u64) -> Option<(u64, u64)> {
+    let mask = page_size() - 1;
+    let first = addr.addr().to_u64() & !mask;
+    let length = len.checked_add(mask)? & !mask;
+    first.checked_add(length)?;
+    Some((first, length))
+}
+
+/// A pointer to `at` in the process's address space.
+pub(super) fn ptr(at: u64) -> *mut c_void {
+    std::ptr::with_exposed_provenance_mut(at.to_usize())
+}
+
+/// `VmProt`'s bits as a page protection: read and write, read alone, or no access.
+fn page_protection(prot: i32) -> u32 {
+    if prot & WRITE != 0 {
+        api::PAGE_READWRITE
+    } else if prot & READ != 0 {
+        api::PAGE_READONLY
+    } else {
+        api::PAGE_NOACCESS
+    }
+}
+
+/// A Mach answer for the system's error of a refused call, which `what` names, logged: want of
+/// memory, commit or address space is `KERN_NO_SPACE`.
+fn refused(what: &str) -> KernReturn {
+    let error = std::io::Error::last_os_error();
+    let kr = match error.raw_os_error() {
+        Some(
+            api::ERROR_NOT_ENOUGH_MEMORY | api::ERROR_OUTOFMEMORY | api::ERROR_COMMITMENT_LIMIT,
+        ) => NO_SPACE,
+        Some(ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER) => INVALID_ARGUMENT,
+        Some(ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED) => PROTECTION_FAILURE,
+        Some(ERROR_INVALID_ADDRESS) => INVALID_ADDRESS,
+        _ => FAILURE,
+    };
+    log::error!(target: "reims_vgpu_vulkan::os", "{what}: the system refused it: {error}");
+    kr
+}
+
+/// The death of a call that left a range of the library's showing nothing and could not put back
+/// what it showed: `call` the system's call that failed, with its error. The range's pages are
+/// read and written by the guest's objects, so no answer of the call's stands in for them.
+fn cannot_restore(call: &'static str) -> ! {
+    let error = std::io::Error::last_os_error();
+    fatal_with_detail(
+        DeathId::Platform {
+            kind: DeathKind::Abort,
+            call,
+        },
+        &format!(
+            "the system refused {call} of a range whose view was unmapped to change part of it \
+             ({error}), so its pages cannot be mapped back"
+        ),
+    )
+}
+
+/// A new section of `length` bytes of zeros, backed by the page file, which maps views for
+/// reading and writing. Where the system gives none, for want of commit, `KERN_NO_SPACE`, the
+/// host's memory running out (`crate::answer::out_of_host_memory`): counted, its line naming the
+/// error, and in strict mode the process ends at the call.
+pub(super) fn section(length: u64) -> Result<Arc<OwnedHandle>, KernReturn> {
+    let (Ok(high), Ok(low)) = (
+        u32::try_from(length >> 32),
+        u32::try_from(length & u64::from(u32::MAX)),
+    ) else {
+        return Err(NO_SPACE);
+    };
+    // SAFETY: an unnamed section of the page file (`INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`), with no security
+    // attributes: the call reads no memory of the caller's.
+    let handle = unsafe {
+        api::CreateFileMappingW(
+            std::ptr::without_provenance_mut(usize::MAX),
+            std::ptr::null(),
+            api::PAGE_READWRITE,
+            high,
+            low,
+            std::ptr::null(),
+        )
+    };
+    if handle.is_null() {
+        let error = std::io::Error::last_os_error();
+        crate::answer::out_of_host_memory("CreateFileMappingW", || {
+            format!(
+                "the system gave no section of {length:#x} bytes for the library's memory \
+                 ({error}): its bytes are charged to the system's commit, of RAM and page files, \
+                 as it is made; Mach's calls charge none on macOS"
+            )
+        });
+        return Err(NO_SPACE);
+    }
+    // SAFETY: a handle just made, owned from here on.
+    Ok(Arc::new(unsafe { OwnedHandle::from_raw_handle(handle) }))
+}
+
+/// A fresh placeholder of `length` bytes, anywhere: its first byte, a multiple of the allocation
+/// granularity.
+fn reserve(length: u64) -> Result<u64, KernReturn> {
+    // SAFETY: a reservation of address space no one holds; nothing is committed or read.
+    let base = unsafe {
+        api::VirtualAlloc2(
+            std::ptr::null_mut(),
+            std::ptr::null_mut(),
+            length.to_usize(),
+            api::MEM_RESERVE | api::MEM_RESERVE_PLACEHOLDER,
+            api::PAGE_NOACCESS,
+            std::ptr::null_mut(),
+            0,
+        )
+    };
+    if base.is_null() {
+        return Err(refused("a reservation of address space"));
+    }
+    Ok(base.addr().to_u64())
+}
+
+/// Maps `length` bytes of `backing`'s section, with protection `prot`, in place of the
+/// placeholder of exactly those bytes at `at`.
+fn map(at: u64, length: u64, backing: &Backing, prot: i32) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+    // A view is mapped readable or writable; one with no access is protected so once mapped.
+    let mapped_as = page_protection(prot | READ);
+    // SAFETY: the placeholder at `at` is the library's own, of exactly `length` bytes, and the
+    // view that takes its place is of a section the table holds open.
+    let mapped = unsafe {
+        api::MapViewOfFile3(
+            backing.file.as_raw_handle(),
+            api::GetCurrentProcess(),
+            ptr(at),
+            backing.offset,
+            length.to_usize(),
+            api::MEM_REPLACE_PLACEHOLDER,
+            mapped_as,
+            std::ptr::null_mut(),
+            0,
+        )
+    };
+    if mapped.is_null() {
+        return Err(refused("a view of a section"));
+    }
+    if prot & READ == 0 && !protect(at, length, prot) {
+        let kr = refused("a view's protection");
+        unmap(at);
+        return Err(kr);
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+/// Unmaps the view at `at`, one the table holds, leaving a placeholder of its bytes. A refusal
+/// is a death: the table would name a view the kernel does not have.
+fn unmap(at: u64) {
+    // SAFETY: the view is the library's own, which its caller no longer names in the table.
+    let unmapped = unsafe {
+        api::UnmapViewOfFile2(
+            api::GetCurrentProcess(),
+            ptr(at),
+            api::MEM_PRESERVE_PLACEHOLDER,
+        )
+    };
+    if unmapped == 0 {
+        cannot_restore("UnmapViewOfFile2");
+    }
+}
+
+/// Cuts the placeholder that starts at `at` in two after `length` bytes.
+fn split(at: u64, length: u64) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+    let kind = api::MEM_RELEASE | api::MEM_PRESERVE_PLACEHOLDER;
+    // SAFETY: a placeholder of the library's own, which stays reserved: only its extent changes.
+    if unsafe { api::VirtualFree(ptr(at), length.to_usize(), kind) } == 0 {
+        return Err(refused("a cut of a placeholder"));
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+/// Joins the placeholders that make up `length` bytes from `at`, which were cut from one, into
+/// one again. A refusal is a death: what the range showed is mapped back in one placeholder.
+fn coalesce(at: u64, length: u64) {
+    let kind = api::MEM_RELEASE | api::MEM_COALESCE_PLACEHOLDERS;
+    // SAFETY: placeholders of the library's own, which stay reserved: only their extents change.
+    if unsafe { api::VirtualFree(ptr(at), length.to_usize(), kind) } == 0 {
+        cannot_restore("VirtualFree(MEM_COALESCE_PLACEHOLDERS)");
+    }
+}
+
+/// Frees the placeholder that starts at `at`: its address space is the system's again. A
+/// refusal is logged, and leaves address space reserved that nothing uses.
+fn release(at: u64) {
+    // SAFETY: a placeholder of the library's own, which nothing maps or names any more.
+    if unsafe { api::VirtualFree(ptr(at), 0, api::MEM_RELEASE) } == 0 {
+        log::error!(
+            target: "reims_vgpu_vulkan::os",
+            "the placeholder at {at:#x} stays reserved: {}",
+            std::io::Error::last_os_error()
+        );
+    }
+}
+
+/// Sets the protection of `length` bytes from `at`, which lie in one view, to `prot`: whether
+/// the system did.
+fn protect(at: u64, length: u64, prot: i32) -> bool {
+    let mut old = 0u32;
+    // SAFETY: pages of one view of the library's own; a later access the protection forbids
+    // faults (a process death), which is the caller's decision.
+    unsafe { api::VirtualProtect(ptr(at), length.to_usize(), page_protection(prot), &mut old) != 0 }
+}
+
+/// Bytes to map as one view: `first..end` shows `backing`'s section from its offset, with
+/// protection `prot`.
+#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
+struct Part {
+    first: u64,
+    end: u64,
+    backing: Backing,
+    prot: i32,
+}
+
+impl Part {
+    /// The part of a run from `first`.
+    fn of_run(first: u64, run: &Run) -> Part {
+        Part {
+            first,
+            end: run.end,
+            backing: run.backing.clone(),
+            prot: run.prot,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The parts of `pieces`, which lie from `target`, that lie in `within`, cut to it, in order.
+fn parts_within(target: u64, pieces: &[Piece], within: (u64, u64)) -> Vec<Part> {
+    (pieces.iter())
+        .filter_map(|piece| {
+            let start = target + piece.at;
+            let (first, end) = (start.max(within.0), (start + piece.length).min(within.1));
+            (first < end).then(|| Part {
+                first,
+                end,
+                backing: Backing {
+                    file: Arc::clone(&piece.backing.file),
+                    offset: piece.backing.offset + (first - start),
+                },
+                prot: piece.prot,
+            })
+        })
+        .collect()
+}
+
+/// Maps `parts`, which follow on and cover the one placeholder `hole`, each as a view of its
+/// own, the placeholder cut for each from its start, and records them. All or nothing: after a
+/// refusal the hole is one placeholder again.
+fn fill(table: &mut Table, hole: (u64, u64), parts: &[Part]) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+    let mut mapped = 0;
+    let mut cut = false;
+    let mut made = Ok(());
+    for (i, part) in parts.iter().enumerate() {
+        let length = part.end - part.first;
+        if i + 1 < parts.len() {
+            made = split(part.first, length);
+            cut |= made.is_ok();
+        }
+        if made.is_ok() {
+            made = map(part.first, length, &part.backing, part.prot);
+        }
+        if made.is_err() {
+            break;
+        }
+        mapped += 1;
+    }
+    if let Err(kr) = made {
+        for part in &parts[..mapped] {
+            unmap(part.first);
+        }
+        if cut {
+            coalesce(hole.0, hole.1 - hole.0);
+        }
+        return Err(kr);
+    }
+    for part in parts {
+        table.insert(
+            part.first,
+            Run {
+                end: part.end,
+                backing: part.backing.clone(),
+                prot: part.prot,
+                view: (part.first, part.end),
+            },
+        );
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+/// Unmaps the views that fill `hole`, each a run of its own ([`fill`]), and forgets them: the
+/// hole is one placeholder again.
+fn empty(table: &mut Table, hole: (u64, u64)) {
+    let views = table.views_meeting(hole.0, hole.1);
+    for &(at, _) in &views {
+        unmap(at);
+    }
+    table.remove(hole.0, hole.1);
+    if views.len() > 1 {
+        coalesce(hole.0, hole.1 - hole.0);
+    }
+}
+
+/// Unmaps what `first..end` shows, every byte of which lies in a run: the placeholders left, in
+/// order, one for what of each of the kernel's views lay in the range. A view the range cuts is
+/// unmapped whole, and what of it lies outside the range mapped again, each run a view of its
+/// own with its protection (module docs): a refusal there is a death ([`cannot_restore`]).
+fn carve(table: &mut Table, first: u64, end: u64) -> Vec<(u64, u64)> {
+    let views = table.views_meeting(first, end);
+    let mut holes = Vec::with_capacity(views.len());
+    for (view_first, view_end) in views {
+        let hole = (view_first.max(first), view_end.min(end));
+        let kept: Vec<Part> = (table.runs_in(view_first, hole.0).iter())
+            .chain(table.runs_in(hole.1, view_end).iter())
+            .map(|(at, run)| Part::of_run(*at, run))
+            .collect();
+        unmap(view_first);
+        table.remove(view_first, view_end);
+        // The view's placeholder, cut from its start: the runs kept before the hole, the hole,
+        // and the runs kept after it.
+        let before = kept.iter().take_while(|part| part.end <= hole.0).count();
+        let mut segments: Vec<(u64, u64, Option<&Part>)> = Vec::with_capacity(kept.len() + 1);
+        segments.extend(kept[..before].iter().map(|p| (p.first, p.end, Some(p))));
+        segments.push((hole.0, hole.1, None));
+        segments.extend(kept[before..].iter().map(|p| (p.first, p.end, Some(p))));
+        let last = segments.len() - 1;
+        for (i, (at, to, part)) in segments.into_iter().enumerate() {
+            if i < last && split(at, to - at).is_err() {
+                cannot_restore("VirtualFree(MEM_PRESERVE_PLACEHOLDER)");
+            }
+            if let Some(part) = part
+                && fill(table, (at, to), std::slice::from_ref(part)).is_err()
+            {
+                cannot_restore("MapViewOfFile3");
+            }
+        }
+        holes.push(hole);
+    }
+    holes
+}
+
+/// Maps `pieces`, which follow on from `target` and cover `length` bytes, over what those bytes
+/// show, and records them (module docs): `KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS`, nothing changed, where a byte
+/// of the target lies in no run. All or nothing: after a piece the system refuses, what the
+/// target showed is mapped back, the same pages, and the call fails with its answer.
+fn replace(
+    table: &mut Table,
+    target: u64,
+    length: u64,
+    pieces: &[Piece],
+) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+    let end = target.checked_add(length).ok_or(INVALID_ARGUMENT)?;
+    if !table.covered(target, end) {
+        log::debug!(
+            target: "reims_vgpu_vulkan::os",
+            "a map over {length:#x} bytes at {target:#x}: not all in a range the library mapped"
+        );
+        return Err(INVALID_ADDRESS);
+    }
+    let old: Vec<Part> = (table.runs_in(target, end).iter())
+        .map(|(at, run)| Part::of_run(*at, run))
+        .collect();
+    let holes = carve(table, target, end);
+    for (done, &hole) in holes.iter().enumerate() {
+        let Err(kr) = fill(table, hole, &parts_within(target, pieces, hole)) else {
+            continue;
+        };
+        for &filled in &holes[..done] {
+            empty(table, filled);
+        }
+        for &hole in &holes {
+            let was: Vec<Part> = (old.iter())
+                .filter(|part| hole.0 <= part.first && part.end <= hole.1)
+                .cloned()
+                .collect();
+            if fill(table, hole, &was).is_err() {
+                cannot_restore("MapViewOfFile3");
+            }
+        }
+        return Err(kr);
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+/// Maps `pieces`, which follow on from `target` and cover `length` bytes of a range the library
+/// mapped, over what it shows ([`replace`]), each with its own protection: an import's twin's
+/// (`super::import`).
+pub(super) fn map_pieces(target: u64, length: u64, pieces: &[Piece]) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+    replace(&mut files::table(), target, length, pieces)
+}
+
+/// A fresh range of `length` bytes, whole pages, of zeros: a view of a section of its own over
+/// a placeholder of its own, read and write, recorded as a run (module docs). Its first byte.
+fn allocate(length: u64) -> Result<u64, KernReturn> {
+    let backing = Backing {
+        file: section(length)?,
+        offset: 0,
+    };
+    let first = reserve(length)?;
+    let mut table = files::table();
+    let part = Part {
+        first,
+        end: first + length,
+        backing,
+        prot: READ_WRITE,
+    };
+    if let Err(kr) = fill(
+        &mut table,
+        (part.first, part.end),
+        std::slice::from_ref(&part),
+    ) {
+        drop(table);
+        release(first);
+        return Err(kr);
+    }
+    Ok(first)
+}
+
+/// A fresh range of `len` bytes, rounded up to whole pages, of zeros, that a view can be made of:
+/// on Windows any range of [`WindowsVm`]'s `allocate_anywhere`, a view of a section of its own
+/// (module docs). Freed by `deallocate`, as any other range. The block of zeros a detached object
+/// reads, and the tests' stand-in for guest memory, are such ranges.
+pub(crate) fn allocate_aliasable(len: u64) -> Result<HostAddr, KernReturn> {
+    WindowsVm::new().allocate_anywhere(len)
+}
+
+/// Memory of the library's own that a view can be made of and that only its holder frees: a view
+/// of a new section, read and write, recorded as a run and registered in the backing table
+/// ([`files::Registration`]), so that `deallocate` refuses it. An IOSurface stand-in's memory.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+pub(crate) struct OwnMemory {
+    first: u64,
+    length: u64,
+    registration: Option<files::Registration>,
+}
+
+impl OwnMemory {
+    /// `len` bytes, rounded up to whole pages, of zeros; `None` for none, or where the host
+    /// gives no section or no address space, as an IOSurface's creation answers nil when memory
+    /// runs out.
+    pub(crate) fn new(len: u64) -> Option<OwnMemory> {
+        let (_, length) = pages(HostAddr::NULL, len)?;
+        if length == 0 {
+            return None;
+        }
+        let first = allocate(length).ok()?;
+        let backing = files::pieces(first, length)?.into_iter().next()?.backing;
+        Some(OwnMemory {
+            first,
+            length,
+            registration: Some(files::Registration::new(first, length, backing)),
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// The first byte.
+    pub(crate) fn first(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.first
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for OwnMemory {
+    /// Drops the registration, then the mapping, through the platform's VM: pages a device's
+    /// import still holds stay mapped until the runtime has freed it, as Vulkan requires of
+    /// imported host memory (`VK_EXT_external_memory_host`), and go as the import's hold does
+    /// ([`crate::remap::UnmapHold`]). A region's last reference may go in a no-copy buffer's
+    /// deallocator (the scanout's, `reims-vgpu`'s `graphics::sink`), which the runtime runs at the
+    /// buffer's release, before it frees the buffer's import of the region's pages.
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        drop(self.registration.take());
+        let at = HostAddr::from_addr(self.first.to_usize());
+        let _ = crate::remap::VulkanVm::new().deallocate(at, self.length);
+    }
+}
+
+/// A graphics device's region (BAR 2) in memory of the library's own ([`OwnMemory`]): a view of a
+/// section of the page file, zeros, page-aligned, read and write, which a device imports by its
+/// pointer (`VK_EXT_external_memory_host`) and a view can be made of, as Linux's is a shared
+/// mapping of a memfd. Its bytes are charged to the system's commit as it is made (1 GiB for a
+/// region of that size), and a page takes memory once it is touched.
+///
+/// Private pages of `VirtualAlloc` would import alike, but the region is freed through the
+/// platform's VM, which unmaps what of it no import holds and the rest as each hold goes
+/// ([`crate::remap::UnmapHold`]): `VirtualFree` frees such an allocation only whole.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+pub struct RegionMemory(OwnMemory);
+
+impl RegionMemory {
+    /// `len` bytes of zeros, rounded up to whole pages; `None` where the host gives no section
+    /// or no address space.
+    pub fn new(len: u64) -> Option<RegionMemory> {
+        OwnMemory::new(len).map(RegionMemory)
+    }
+
+    /// Its first byte.
+    pub fn base(&self) -> *mut u8 {
+        ptr(self.0.first()).cast()
+    }
+}
+
+/// `runs` remapped one at a time, as `HostVm::remap_runs_shared_overwrite` describes.
+fn one_at_a_time(
+    vm: &WindowsVm,
+    dst: HostAddr,
+    runs: &[(HostAddr, u64)],
+) -> Result<(), (usize, KernReturn)> {
+    let mut at = dst;
+    for (i, &(src, len)) in runs.iter().enumerate() {
+        vm.remap_shared_overwrite(at, src, len)
+            .map_err(|kr| (i, kr))?;
+        at = at.wrapping_add(len);
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+/// Whether every page of `length` bytes from `first` is committed and readable, as the system
+/// answers for each region of them (`VirtualQuery`), reading none of them.
+fn query_readable(first: u64, length: u64) -> bool {
+    regions(first, length, |info| {
+        info.state == api::MEM_COMMIT
+            && info.protection & (api::PAGE_NOACCESS | api::PAGE_GUARD) == 0
+    })
+}
+
+/// Whether `allows` answers yes for every region of the `length` bytes from `first`, as
+/// `VirtualQuery` describes each in turn; no where the system describes none.
+fn regions(first: u64, length: u64, allows: impl Fn(&api::MemoryInfo) -> bool) -> bool {
+    let Some(end) = first.checked_add(length) else {
+        return false;
+    };
+    let mut at = first;
+    while at < end {
+        let mut info = std::mem::MaybeUninit::<api::MemoryInfo>::zeroed();
+        let size = size_of::<api::MemoryInfo>();
+        // SAFETY: a query of an address, which reads nothing there and fills the structure given.
+        let filled = unsafe { api::VirtualQuery(ptr(at), info.as_mut_ptr(), size) };
+        if filled != size {
+            return false;
+        }
+        // SAFETY: the query filled the whole structure.
+        let info = unsafe { info.assume_init() };
+        let next = (info.base.addr().to_u64()).saturating_add(info.region_size.to_u64());
+        if !allows(&info) || next <= at {
+            return false;
+        }
+        at = next;
+    }
+    true
+}
+
+/// Mach VM's calls on Windows (module docs).
+#[derive(Debug, Default)]
+pub struct WindowsVm;
+
+impl WindowsVm {
+    /// The process's HostVm.
+    pub fn new() -> WindowsVm {
+        WindowsVm
+    }
+
+    /// Whether every page of the `len` bytes at `addr` is mapped with read access: by the
+    /// backing table's protections where it knows every byte, else as the system describes the
+    /// pages (`VirtualQuery`: committed, with access and no guard), reading none of them. A `len`
+    /// of 0 is `true`.
+    pub fn readable(&self, addr: HostAddr, len: u64) -> bool {
+        if len == 0 {
+            return true;
+        }
+        let Some((first, length)) = pages(addr, len) else {
+            return false;
+        };
+        files::readable(first, length).unwrap_or_else(|| query_readable(first, length))
+    }
+}
+
+impl HostVm for WindowsVm {
+    fn page_size(&self) -> u64 {
+        page_size()
+    }
+
+    fn page_shift(&self) -> u32 {
+        page_size().trailing_zeros()
+    }
+
+    /// A view of a section of its own anywhere; a length of 0 answers address 0, as Mach's does.
+    fn allocate_anywhere(&self, len: u64) -> Result<HostAddr, KernReturn> {
+        let Some((_, length)) = pages(HostAddr::NULL, len) else {
+            return Err(NO_SPACE);
+        };
+        if length == 0 {
+            return Ok(HostAddr::NULL);
+        }
+        let at = allocate(length)?;
+        Ok(HostAddr::from_addr(at.to_usize()))
+    }
+
+    /// A view of a fresh section over the pages of the range's length from the page of its
+    /// address (module docs).
+    fn allocate_fixed_overwrite(&self, addr: HostAddr, len: u64) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+        let (first, length) = length_pages(addr, len).ok_or(NO_SPACE)?;
+        if length == 0 {
+            return Ok(());
+        }
+        let zeros = Piece {
+            at: 0,
+            length,
+            backing: Backing {
+                file: section(length)?,
+                offset: 0,
+            },
+            prot: READ_WRITE,
+        };
+        replace(
+            &mut files::table(),
+            first,
+            length,
+            std::slice::from_ref(&zeros),
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// The source's pages of the length, from the page of its first byte, mapped from their
+    /// sections over as many from `dst`'s page, each with the protection the source has there:
+    /// that page's first byte, which Mach writes back (module docs).
+    fn remap_shared_overwrite(
+        &self,
+        dst: HostAddr,
+        src: HostAddr,
+        len: u64,
+    ) -> Result<HostAddr, KernReturn> {
+        if len == 0 {
+            return Err(INVALID_ARGUMENT);
+        }
+        let (target, length) = length_pages(dst, len).ok_or(INVALID_ARGUMENT)?;
+        let (source, _) = length_pages(src, len).ok_or(INVALID_ADDRESS)?;
+        let mut table = files::table();
+        let mut pieces = Vec::new();
+        if !table.pieces_into(source, length, 0, &mut pieces) {
+            log::debug!(
+                target: "reims_vgpu_vulkan::os",
+                "remap of {length:#x} bytes at {source:#x}: no section behind them"
+            );
+            return Err(INVALID_ADDRESS);
+        }
+        replace(&mut table, target, length, &pieces)?;
+        Ok(HostAddr::from_addr(target.to_usize()))
+    }
+
+    /// The runs' pages mapped as one view, whole pages each, resolved and mapped under one hold
+    /// of the table: each stretch of them that follows on in a section a view of the kernel's. A
+    /// run with no section behind a byte is refused after the runs before it are mapped, as one
+    /// remap at a time leaves them; a map the system refuses leaves none of them mapped, answered
+    /// at the first run. Runs that are not whole pages from a page, or a view not from one, are
+    /// remapped one at a time.
+    fn remap_runs_shared_overwrite(
+        &self,
+        dst: HostAddr,
+        runs: &[(HostAddr, u64)],
+    ) -> Result<(), (usize, KernReturn)> {
+        let page = page_size();
+        let on_pages = |at: HostAddr| at.addr().to_u64().is_multiple_of(page);
+        if !on_pages(dst)
+            || (runs.iter())
+                .any(|&(src, len)| len == 0 || !len.is_multiple_of(page) || !on_pages(src))
+        {
+            return one_at_a_time(self, dst, runs);
+        }
+        let target = dst.addr().to_u64();
+        let mut table = files::table();
+        let mut pieces = Vec::new();
+        let mut length = 0u64;
+        let mut unresolved = None;
+        for (i, &(src, len)) in runs.iter().enumerate() {
+            let source = src.addr().to_u64();
+            let before = pieces.len();
+            if !table.pieces_into(source, len, length, &mut pieces) {
+                // What the run added before the byte with no section goes: its own pieces, and
+                // what it joined to the last piece of the runs before it.
+                pieces.truncate(before);
+                if let Some(last) = pieces.last_mut() {
+                    last.length = last.length.min(length - last.at);
+                }
+                log::debug!(
+                    target: "reims_vgpu_vulkan::os",
+                    "remap of {len:#x} bytes at {source:#x}: no section behind them"
+                );
+                unresolved = Some(i);
+                break;
+            }
+            length += len;
+        }
+        if length > 0 {
+            replace(&mut table, target, length, &pieces).map_err(|kr| (0, kr))?;
+        }
+        unresolved.map_or(Ok(()), |i| Err((i, INVALID_ADDRESS)))
+    }
+
+    /// `VirtualProtect` of the range's pages, a maximum protection as Mach's: lowered, never
+    /// raised; none for no bytes (module docs).
+    fn protect_max(&self, addr: HostAddr, len: u64, prot: VmProt) -> Result<(), KernReturn> {
+        if len == 0 {
+            return Ok(());
+        }
+        let (first, length) = pages(addr, len).ok_or(INVALID_ARGUMENT)?;
+        let end = first + length;
+        let bits = prot.raw() & READ_WRITE;
+        let mut table = files::table();
+        if !table.covered(first, end) {
+            return Err(INVALID_ADDRESS);
+        }
+        // The runs the protect changes; one that would gain an access refuses it, nothing
+        // changed.
+        let mut changes = false;
+        let mut at = first;
+        while let Some((_, run)) = table.run_at(at).filter(|_| at < end) {
+            if bits & !run.prot != 0 {
+                return Err(PROTECTION_FAILURE);
+            }
+            changes |= run.prot != bits;
+            at = run.end;
+        }
+        // Core protects each mapping it made once its views are in, read and write as the views
+        // already are unless it is read-only: no call where the views have the protection.
+        if !changes {
+            return Ok(());
+        }
+        for (at, run) in table.runs_in(first, end) {
+            if run.prot == bits {
+                continue;
+            }
+            if !protect(at, run.end - at, bits) {
+                return Err(refused("protect_max"));
+            }
+            table.set_prot(at, run.end, bits);
+        }
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
+    /// Unmaps what of the range's pages the library mapped, which show no section from then on,
+    /// and frees their address space; none for no bytes (module docs). A range of memory the
+    /// library registered and does not free here (the guest's RAM, a stand-in's memory) is
+    /// refused, unmapped by nothing. It goes a stretch of runs at a time ([`DEALLOCATE_RUNS`]).
+    fn deallocate(&self, addr: HostAddr, len: u64) -> KernReturn {
+        if len == 0 {
+            return KernReturn::SUCCESS;
+        }
+        let Some((first, length)) = pages(addr, len) else {
+            return INVALID_ARGUMENT;
+        };
+        // `pages` answered, so the range ends inside the address space.
+        let end = first + length;
+        let mut at = first;
+        while at < end {
+            let mut table = files::table();
+            if table.meets_registration(first, end) {
+                log::error!(
+                    target: "reims_vgpu_vulkan::os",
+                    "deallocate of {length:#x} bytes at {first:#x}: registered memory, not unmapped"
+                );
+                return INVALID_ARGUMENT;
+            }
+            let Some((from, to)) = table.covered_stretch(at, end, DEALLOCATE_RUNS) else {
+                break;
+            };
+            for hole in carve(&mut table, from, to) {
+                release(hole.0);
+            }
+            at = to;
+        }
+        KernReturn::SUCCESS
+    }
+}
+
+#[cfg(test)]
+pub(crate) mod tests {
+    use super::*;
+
+    fn vm() -> WindowsVm {
+        WindowsVm::new()
+    }
+
+    fn read(a: HostAddr, off: u64) -> u8 {
+        // SAFETY: test memory this test mapped readable.
+        unsafe { a.wrapping_add(off).as_ptr::<u8>().read_volatile() }
+    }
+
+    fn write(a: HostAddr, off: u64, v: u8) {
+        // SAFETY: test memory this test mapped writable.
+        unsafe { a.wrapping_add(off).as_ptr::<u8>().write_volatile(v) }
+    }
+
+    /// The views of the kernel's and the runs the table holds in `pages` pages from `at`.
+    fn views_and_runs(at: HostAddr, pages: u64) -> (usize, usize) {
+        let first = at.addr().to_u64();
+        let end = first + pages * PAGE;
+        (
+            files::views_between(first, end),
+            files::runs_between(first, end),
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// The regions the system describes in `pages` pages from `at`, each its state and
+    /// protection (`VirtualQuery`).
+    fn kernel_regions(at: HostAddr, pages: u64) -> Vec<(u64, u32, u32)> {
+        let first = at.addr().to_u64();
+        let found = std::cell::RefCell::new(Vec::new());
+        regions(first, pages * PAGE, |info| {
+            let from = info.base.addr().to_u64().max(first);
+            found
+                .borrow_mut()
+                .push(((from - first) / PAGE, info.state, info.protection));
+            true
+        });
+        found.into_inner()
+    }
+
+    /// The host's page, which the tests lay their pages out by: x86-64's 4 KiB.
+    pub(crate) const PAGE: u64 = 0x1000;
+
+    /// Whether every page of `len` bytes from `at` is writable now, as the system describes
+    /// them (`VirtualQuery`): committed, read and write.
+    pub(crate) fn writable(at: HostAddr, len: u64) -> bool {
+        let Some((first, length)) = pages(at, len) else {
+            return false;
+        };
+        regions(first, length, |info| {
+            info.state == api::MEM_COMMIT && info.protection == api::PAGE_READWRITE
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// Whether the page at `at` is mapped at all: committed, as the system describes it, where a
+    /// placeholder is reserved and a hole free.
+    pub(crate) fn mapped(at: HostAddr) -> bool {
+        let first = at.addr().to_u64() & !(PAGE - 1);
+        regions(first, PAGE, |info| info.state == api::MEM_COMMIT)
+    }
+
+    /// The host's page is the tests', 4 KiB, not the allocation granularity
+    #[test]
+    fn the_page_is_4_kib() {
+        assert_eq!(page_size(), PAGE);
+        assert_eq!(vm().page_shift(), 12);
+    }
+
+    /// A fresh range is whole pages of zeros, read and write, at a multiple of the allocation
+    /// granularity; none for no bytes; and it is unmapped by its deallocate, in parts too
+    #[test]
+    fn a_fresh_range_is_zeros_until_it_is_deallocated() {
+        let v = vm();
+        assert_eq!(v.allocate_anywhere(0), Ok(HostAddr::NULL));
+        let a = v.allocate_anywhere(3 * PAGE + 1).unwrap();
+        assert_eq!(a.addr().to_u64() % 0x1_0000, 0, "a multiple of 64 KiB");
+        assert!((0..4).all(|i| mapped(a.wrapping_add(i * PAGE))));
+        assert!(!mapped(a.wrapping_add(4 * PAGE)), "whole pages, no more");
+        assert!((0..4 * PAGE).all(|off| read(a, off) == 0));
+        assert!(writable(a, 4 * PAGE));
+        write(a, 0, 1);
+        write(a, 4 * PAGE - 1, 2);
+        assert_eq!((read(a, 0), read(a, 4 * PAGE - 1)), (1, 2));
+        assert_eq!(views_and_runs(a, 4), (1, 1));
+        // Its second page, by a byte of it: the pages either side stay, with their bytes.
+        assert!(v.deallocate(a.wrapping_add(PAGE + 7), 1).is_success());
+        let now: Vec<bool> = (0..4).map(|i| mapped(a.wrapping_add(i * PAGE))).collect();
+        assert_eq!(now, [true, false, true, true]);
+        assert_eq!((read(a, 0), read(a, 4 * PAGE - 1)), (1, 2));
+        assert_eq!(views_and_runs(a, 4), (2, 2));
+        // The whole range, the hole included.
+        assert!(v.deallocate(a, 4 * PAGE).is_success());
+        assert!((0..4).all(|i| !mapped(a.wrapping_add(i * PAGE))));
+        assert_eq!(views_and_runs(a, 4), (0, 0));
+        assert!(
+            v.deallocate(a, 4 * PAGE).is_success(),
+            "again, over nothing"
+        );
+    }
