 refusal logs a line saying which, and no file is duplicated until every entry has passed.
 
+Windows hosts pass `memory_sections` instead of `memory_files`: one `RvgMemoryRangeSection`
+per range, naming a writable section handle, its total length and the range's byte offset.
+The library validates the descriptors and writable views before duplicating the handles;
+its copies keep the section alive until the map and its views are released. The caller keeps
+the original RAM mapped for the device's lifetime. A zero handle names no backing, so a view
+of those pages is refused and the guest falls back to a copy. Sections on another OS, sections
+without a map, and sections combined with files are invalid arguments. Older 40- and 48-byte
+graphics configs read the appended section pointer as NULL; sizes that end inside the pointer
+are refused. QEMU's `reims-graphics-pci,guest-memory=off` suppresses the map for copy-mode comparisons.
+
